('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 1, 'Easy',
 'Which situation requires the sine law rather than the cosine law to solve a triangle?', 1,
 '[{"text": "You know all three side lengths (SSS)", "feedback": "Knowing all three sides with no angles is exactly when the cosine law is needed — the sine law needs at least one known angle-side pair."}, {"text": "You know two angles and one side (AAS or ASA)", "feedback": "Correct. The sine law needs a complete angle-side pair (a known angle and the side directly opposite it) somewhere in the triangle, which two known angles and one side always provides."}, {"text": "You know two sides and the angle between them (SAS)", "feedback": "The angle here isn''t opposite either of the two known sides, so there''s no complete pair for the sine law — that''s a cosine law situation."}, {"text": "You know two sides and the angle between them, and none of the angles", "feedback": "This describes SAS, which is exactly the situation the cosine law is designed to solve, not the sine law."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-vs-cosine-law-conditions'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 2, 'Easy',
 'In the sine law, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, what must a side and the angle paired with it in the same fraction have in common?', 1,
 '[{"text": "They must both be the largest in the triangle", "feedback": "The sine law pairs a side with an angle regardless of size — the key requirement is about their position in the triangle, not which is largest."}, {"text": "The angle must be directly opposite that side", "feedback": "Correct. Each fraction in the sine law pairs a side with the angle sitting directly across from it in the triangle."}, {"text": "They must be adjacent to each other", "feedback": "The angle paired with a side in the sine law is the one opposite it, not one of the two angles touching that side."}, {"text": "The angle must be acute", "feedback": "The sine law works with any angle, acute or obtuse — what matters is that the angle is opposite the paired side, not its size."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-formula-structure'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 3, 'Easy',
 'You know two angles of a triangle and one side. Which law should you use to find a missing side?', 0,
 '[{"text": "The sine law", "feedback": "Correct. Knowing two angles and one side gives a complete angle-side pair (the known side and its opposite angle), which the sine law needs."}, {"text": "The cosine law", "feedback": "The cosine law is used when you know two sides and the included angle, or all three sides — not two angles and one side."}, {"text": "The Pythagorean theorem", "feedback": "The Pythagorean theorem only applies to right triangles — there''s no indication this triangle has a right angle."}, {"text": "The quadratic formula", "feedback": "The quadratic formula solves equations of the form ax²+bx+c=0 — it has no role in solving a triangle for a missing side."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-vs-cosine-law-conditions'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 4, 'Easy',
 'In a triangle, angle A = 50° and angle B = 70°. What is angle C?', 2,
 '[{"text": "130°", "feedback": "This adds A and B together without subtracting from 180° — the three angles of a triangle sum to 180°."}, {"text": "50°", "feedback": "This just reuses angle A — find C by subtracting both given angles from 180°."}, {"text": "60°", "feedback": "Correct. The three angles of a triangle always sum to 180°, so C = 180° − 50° − 70° = 60°."}, {"text": "120°", "feedback": "This subtracts only one of the two given angles from 180° — both A and B need to be subtracted."}]'::jsonb,
 'triangle-angle-sum'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 5, 'Easy',
 'In a triangle with sides a, b, c opposite angles A, B, C, which of these is a correctly written sine law equation?', 1,
 '[{"text": "a/sin B = b/sin A", "feedback": "Each side should be paired with its *own* opposite angle — a with A, and b with B, not swapped."}, {"text": "a/sin A = b/sin B", "feedback": "Correct. The sine law pairs each side with the angle directly opposite it: a with A, and b with B."}, {"text": "sin A/a = sin B/c", "feedback": "This mixes side b''s opposite angle (B) with side c''s own letter — each side should be paired only with its own opposite angle."}, {"text": "a × sin A = b × sin B", "feedback": "The sine law is a ratio (division), not a product — each side is divided by the sine of its opposite angle."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-formula-structure'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 6, 'Easy',
 'The sine law relates what quantities?', 0,
 '[
   {"text": "Each side to the sine of its opposite angle", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "The two legs and the hypotenuse", "feedback": "Legs and hypotenuse language belongs to RIGHT triangles. The sine law works on any triangle."},
   {"text": "Each side to the sine of its adjacent angle", "feedback": "The pairing is strictly OPPOSITE: a with A, across the triangle."},
   {"text": "The three angles only", "feedback": "Angles alone come from the 180° sum. The sine law brings the sides in."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 7, 'Medium',
 'In a triangle, angle A = 40°, side a = 10, and angle B = 65°. What is the length of side b, to one decimal place?', 3,
 '[{"text": "b ≈ 10.8", "feedback": "This doesn''t come from a÷sin(A) = b÷sin(B) — recheck by cross-multiplying: b = 10 × sin(65°) ÷ sin(40°)."}, {"text": "b ≈ 6.2", "feedback": "This looks like sin(A) and sin(B) were swapped in the ratio — side b should come out larger than side a, since angle B is bigger than angle A."}, {"text": "b ≈ 15.6", "feedback": "This is larger than the correct value — double check that sin(65°) and sin(40°) were placed correctly in the ratio b = a×sin(B)÷sin(A)."}, {"text": "b ≈ 14.1", "feedback": "Correct. By the sine law, a÷sin(A) = b÷sin(B), so b = 10 × sin(65°) ÷ sin(40°) ≈ 14.1."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-solve-for-side'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 8, 'Medium',
 'In a triangle, angle A = 55°, side a = 12, and angle B = 70°. What is the length of side b, to one decimal place?', 3,
 '[{"text": "b ≈ 10.5", "feedback": "This doesn''t come from a÷sin(A) = b÷sin(B) — recheck by cross-multiplying: b = 12 × sin(70°) ÷ sin(55°)."}, {"text": "b ≈ 8.2", "feedback": "This looks like sin(A) and sin(B) were swapped in the ratio — side b should come out larger than side a, since angle B is bigger than angle A."}, {"text": "b ≈ 16.9", "feedback": "This is larger than the correct value — double check that sin(70°) and sin(55°) were placed correctly in the ratio."}, {"text": "b ≈ 13.8", "feedback": "Correct. By the sine law, a÷sin(A) = b÷sin(B), so b = 12 × sin(70°) ÷ sin(55°) ≈ 13.8."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-solve-for-side'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'sine-law', 9, 'Medium',
 'In a triangle, angle A = 30°, side a = 8, and angle C = 100°. What is the length of side c, to one decimal place?', 1,
 '[{"text": "c ≈ 4.1", "feedback": "This looks like sin(A) and sin(C) were swapped in the ratio — side c should come out larger than side a, since angle C is much bigger than angle A."}, {"text": "c ≈ 15.8", "feedback": "Correct. By the sine law, a÷sin(A) = c÷sin(C), so c = 8 × sin(100°) ÷ sin(30°) ≈ 15.8."}, {"text": "c ≈ 26.7", "feedback": "This is too large — double check that sin(100°) and sin(30°) were placed correctly in the ratio."}, {"text": "c ≈ 8.0", "feedback": "This just reuses side a — apply the sine law ratio to find the actual value of c."}]'::jsonb,
 'sine-law-solve-for-side'),
